Findings
--------
1. BLOCKING — opus.red_evidence (mechanical, reproduced above). No red logs
   for any of the brief's four behaviours; studio/ci/reds/W-023/ does not
   exist (W-020, W-021, W-024, W-025, W-028, W-029 all have one). "Test-first
   with a recorded red" is a standing rule. Upheld from round 1.

2. BLOCKING (NEW) — the suite does not bite on the one thing this opus
   delivers. W-023's entire deliverable is the promotion of
   path.id.unvalidated and path.escapes.officina from `advise` to `block`
   (brief behaviours 1 and 2; the Patron's decree: "BOTH rules re-promote to
   blocking"). Every unit test in rules.test.ts asserts only that a finding
   with the right `rule` id is present or absent — none asserts `level`:

     check(`path.id.unvalidated: blocks opus id "${badId}"`,
           findings.some((f) => f.rule === "path.id.unvalidated"), ...)

   The test names say "blocks"; the assertions do not check that it blocks.
   Reverting both rules to `advise` — i.e. undoing 100% of the opus — leaves
   all 18 path.* tests PASSING and `npm test` at exit 0. The one integration
   assertion that mentions levels is negative ("no BLOCKING path.* finding"),
   so demotion makes it *more* likely to pass, never less. There is no
   executable evidence that this change did anything. Per the QA lex §2, one
   surviving mutation on the opus's own deliverable is enough to fail.
   (Round 1 did not mutate; this is the round-2 finding.)

3. OVERTURNED — round 1's finding 2 (scope creep on the `lessons/` check).
   Round 1 was wrong on the record and, in my judgement, wrong on the merits.

   (a) On the record: round 1 states the brief says only "removed acta
       evidence checks". It does not. studio/briefs/W-023.md line 8 reads
       "remove acta and lessons evidence checks". The removal is named in the
       opus's own spec of record, not undisclosed. Round 1 appears to have
       quoted the commit message ("acta evidence removed per P-004") and
       attributed it to the brief.

   (b) On the merits: P-004's decree is a positive whitelist, not a
       subtraction. The Patron wrote "path.escapes.officina scopes to the
       contractually officina-relative surfaces — opus spec: and gate
       evidence: paths." Once the rule is scoped TO those two surfaces,
       everything not on the list is out by construction; `lessons/` is not
       on the list. The clause "acta evidence hrefs stay with checkLink"
       names acta because acta was the firing site under discussion — it
       explains why acta is safe to drop, it is not an exhaustive list of
       what drops. Reading the decree as "remove the acta block only" would
       require the Patron to have scoped the rule to three surfaces while
       naming two.

   (c) No containment hole. Lessons evidence is not contractually
       officina-contained either: lesson.evidence in rules/process.ts
       resolves each href with existsSync(join(root, href)), which accepts
       ../README.md exactly as acta's checkLink does. A lesson is a
       retrospective artifact of the same nature as an acta digest and cites
       repo-root docs for the same reasons. Keeping the lessons block while
       dropping the acta one would have re-raised the identical
       false-positive class one retro later, against a rule the Patron had
       just narrowed. escapesOfficina is a hygiene rule over the user's own
       trusted tree; it reports and never dereferences. Removing it is right,
       and it is within the decree.

4. ADVISORY — the brief's behaviour 3 ("acta evidence escaping does NOT
   fire") still has no dedicated unit test; coverage rests on the real-studio
   integration assertion, which would pass identically if the code had been
   deleted for a wrong reason, and which depends on studio/acta/
   2026-09-17-kickoff.md keeping its ../README.md href. Upheld from round 1
   at the same severity. Same root cause as finding 2: the path tests probe
   rule ids, never levels and never scoped-out surfaces.

5. ADVISORY — the rules/paths.ts module comment ("acta evidence stays with
   checkLink") is accurate for acta (check.ts:574 does route acta evidence to
   checkLink) but silent on lessons, whose containment is now governed by
   nothing — lesson.evidence tests existence, not containment. An omission,
   not the misstatement round 1 called it. One line would close it.

6. ADVISORY — studio/briefs/W-023.md was created in commit 82245e2, the same
   commit as the implementation, so the spec gate is not independent prior
   authorization for anything. It post-dates the decree and is legitimate as
   the spec of record (see finding 3a), but it cannot also serve as evidence
   that the work was specified before it was written. Compounds finding 1.

7. ADVISORY — gate certificates are stale: tests/lint/types all certify
   tree:5ab03a0b, current tree is tree:1752b551. Nothing in W-023 changed,
   but the certificates no longer name the tree they are read against and
   must be re-run at re-submission.

   Also outstanding and blocking on the opus, outside this review's scope:
   traditio.present — opera/W-023.md is `building` with no handoff.

Disposition
-----------
Failing round 2. The diff itself is correct — the scoping matches the decree,
the promotion matches it, and the real tree is clean at every level. What is
missing is evidence that any of it was built or is defended:

  (a) recorded reds for behaviours 1-4 under studio/ci/reds/W-023/;
  (b) level assertions on the path tests, so that reverting block -> advise
      turns the suite red (this is the opus's whole deliverable, and today
      nothing detects its removal);
  (c) a unit test for behaviour 3 that builds its own acta/ (and lessons/)
      fixture with an out-of-officina evidence href and asserts no
      path.escapes.officina finding — not dependent on studio/acta's shape;
  (d) re-run tests/lint/types certificates against the tree at re-submission.

Round 1's requirement (b) — "brief or petitio authorization for scoping out
lessons/, or restore that check" — is withdrawn. The lessons removal is
authorized by the brief and within P-004. Do not restore it.
